WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
An enzyme can't grab its substrate.

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:06.010
The two meet only when random
motion brings them together.

3
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.200
In 1894, Emil Fischer
pictured the enzyme as a lock,

4
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.480
and its substrate as the key.

5
00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:17.250
That explained why each
enzyme acts on one substrate.

6
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.160
Then, in 1958, Daniel Koshland
proposed something else.

7
00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:26.300
So is an enzyme a rigid
lock waiting for one key,

8
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.450
or does it change shape
as its target arrives?

9
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.990
In this mission, you'll compare the
two models against the evidence.

10
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.900
Then you'll find out why only some collisions
with the active site lead to a reaction.

11
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.550
Your mission: decide whether
an enzyme is a rigid lock,

12
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.320
or changes shape as its target arrives.
